# Tiger Style backlog — findings deferred at 2026-10-22

Scope: the packages in `workspace.metadata.tigerstyle.default_scope` in the root
`Cargo.toml`.

The scan for this record ran one package at a time. The lint stops at the first
package that fails to compile under lint, so a single run over the whole scope
hides every package behind the first failure. The per-package scan found 477
findings in 24 packages.

Within one package the lint also reports in waves: a library that fails type
checking under lint does not report every lint finding, so the `mantle` library
was rescanned after each repair until it compiled with no finding. The gate runs
`cargo check --lib` (`workspace.metadata.tigerstyle.cargo_check_args`), so a
scoped package without a library target, such as the binary-only
`crunch-python`, contributes no lint target.

## What was fixed

Every finding that is not listed under "What was deferred" was fixed in the
source. The categories fixed in place were
assertion density, compound conditions, numeric unit naming, bool naming,
explicit defaults, unchecked unsigned arithmetic, function length, nested
conditionals, parameter count, ambiguous parameters, collection growth, loop
bounds, platform-dependent casts, unchecked narrowing, unchecked division,
panicking calls, silently discarded results, unjustified allows and recursion.

The fixes preserve the accepted and rejected sets, the error codes, the error
strings, the published public APIs, the serde derives and the `r[impl ...]` /
`r[verify ...]` markers. The GPU and FPGA slices were additionally checked
against the pre-change implementation over roughly 30,000 mutated inputs, with
byte-identical results and identical error strings.

## What was deferred

Each deferred finding is scoped by a file-level `#![allow(tigerstyle::...)]`
with a `HARDENING-BACKLOG 2026-10-22` comment naming this file. The
`dylint.toml` lint policy is unchanged.

| File | Category | Finding | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| `crates/crunch-build/src/distributed/resource_quota_store.rs` | `ambient_clock` | Direct system clock read in the quota ledger shell | The read carries a real durable-quota freshness decision. Removing the ambient read would change the admission behaviour, so the finding is scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `crates/crunch-build/src/distributed/resource_policy.rs` | `ambiguous_params`, `too_many_parameters` | Two same-typed arguments, and a parameter count above the limit | The adapter's published entry points take the resource inventory and the lease port by position. Bundling them would change the published signature of the crate. |
| `crates/crunch-verification-bridge-core/src/lib.rs` | `explicit_defaults` | Three `#[serde(default)]` option fields on the published `Observation` DTO | Serde expands each attribute into `Default::default()` for an absent field. Removing the attribute would reject observations that omit an optional identity, changing both the accepted inputs and the published wire format. |
| `crates/crunch-verification-bridge/src/lib.rs` | `ambiguous_params` | The published consume entry points take their profile and receipt path arguments adjacently | Bundling them would change the published signature of the crate. |
| `crates/mantle-cargo-build-bundle-core/src/lib.rs` | `ambiguous_params` | `store_root` takes a path and a prefix adjacently | Bundling them would change the published signature of the crate. |
| `crates/mantle-cargo-unit-plan/src/lib.rs` | `explicit_defaults` | Three `#[serde(default)]` option fields on the published plan bindings | Serde expands each attribute into `Default::default()` for an absent field. Removing the attribute would reject plans that omit an optional binding, changing the accepted inputs and the published wire format. |
| `crates/crunch-fpga-core/src/lib.rs` | `explicit_defaults` | One `#[serde(default)]` option field on the published FPGA report | Serde expands each attribute into `Default::default()` for an absent field. Removing it would reject reports that omit the optional field, changing both the accepted inputs and the published wire format. |
| `crates/crunch-rustc-wrapper/src/lib.rs` | `ambient_clock` | Direct system clock read in the Rust execution permit wait | The read carries the real capacity-wait deadline decision of the daemon. Injecting the clock would change the published daemon entry points, so the finding is scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `crates/crunch-rustc-wrapper/src/slots.rs` | `ambient_clock` | Three direct system clock reads: the lease epoch, the acquire deadline and the deadline transport | The reads carry the real slot-lifetime and timeout decisions of the compile-slot lease. Removing the ambient reads would change the lease timing behaviour, so the finding is scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `crates/crunch-rustc-wrapper/src/cc.rs` | `ambient_clock` | Direct system clock read in the compiler deadline wait | The read carries the real compiler deadline decision of the driver boundary. Injecting the clock would change the published `run_driver` signature or the compiler-supervision semantics, so the finding is scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `crates/crunch-test/src/lib.rs` | `ambient_clock`, `contradictory_time` | The clock reads that turn the caller-supplied timeout into an absolute deadline, and the reads that enforce it and the descendant-teardown deadline | The reads carry the real timeout, cancellation and teardown decisions of a spawned test. Unifying them with the supplied timeout would change the scheduling decision, so the findings are scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `src/bootstrap_effect_port.rs` | `no_unwrap` | Two `.expect()` calls on the planned-effect lookup in `BootstrapPort::call` and `BootstrapPort::call_async` | A capability call for an effect that was never planned is a programming error, and the capability's generic `Result<T, E>` has no error value to return in its place. Returning one instead would turn the abort into an error that callers could handle, which changes observable behaviour, so the finding is scheduled for the standalone hardening pass. |
| `src/elf_dynamic_fixup_core.rs` | `usize_in_public_api` | `plan_dynamic_relocation` takes a `usize` depth and `StringPatch` publishes a `usize` offset | The module is `pub` in the `mantle` library and is consumed by the relocation shell and the reference-scanner and relocation integration tests. Narrowing the values would change the published library API. |
| `src/elf_dynamic_fixup_shell.rs` | `usize_in_public_api` | `relocate_dynamic_file` and `relocate_declared_file` take a `usize` depth and return a `usize` patch count | The module is `pub` in the `mantle` library and is consumed by the relocation integration test. Narrowing the values would change the published library API. |
| `src/source_built_fixed_point_action_controller.rs` | `explicit_defaults` | One `#[serde(default)]` option field, `guard`, on the controller specification | Serde expands the attribute into `Default::default()` for an absent field. Removing it would reject action plans whose controllers omit the optional guard, changing the accepted inputs and the plan wire format. |
| `src/user_profile_cmd/admission.rs` | `too_many_parameters` | `package_from_derivation` takes six parameters | The function is re-exported from the `mantle` library's `user_profile_cmd` module. Bundling the parameters would change the published library API. |

## Why these were recorded rather than forced

Removing any of these findings would change a published public API, change
which inputs are accepted, or change observable failure behaviour. That is a
larger change than a style pass, so the finding is recorded against the
standalone hardening pass instead of being forced through a signature,
wire-format or failure-mode change.

The `HARDENING-BACKLOG` convention is the repository's own. The workspace
Tiger Style hardening pass of 2026-09-09, commit `3f0385b0f`, closed the same
gate with the same mechanism, and its message records that findings were
"fixed or narrowly scoped with recorded reasons".

## Non-claims

This record does not claim that the deferred findings are fixed, that the
affected crates are release eligible, or that a green Tiger Style gate is a
correctness claim about any adapter. It records which findings remain, where
they are, why they remain, and the pass they are scheduled for.
